 ### Ray Generator Network (RGN)
-Used in MEIL-NeRF for incremental learning of NeRF [S27]. During training on task $T$, the RGN $f_T^{RGN}$ learns to map past principal rays from $f_{T-1}^{RGN}$ and current GT principal rays [S27]. During inference, a principal ray is generated using the RGN, and non-principal rays are generated around it using camera intrinsics $K$ to form a cone [S27]. The network architecture consists of 3 hidden layers with 16, 64, and 32 hidden units respectively [S27]. Estimated camera poses accumulate errors over tasks, but the generated approximate rays still effectively direct to the scene of interest near past task cameras, so exact reconstruction of past rays is not required [S27]. Unlike methods that reproduce exact past rays, RGN creates approximate rays directed to the scene of interest, allowing errors under a certain level to reduce the learning burden at a small accuracy cost [S27].
+Used in MEIL-NeRF for incremental learning of NeRF [S27]. During training on task $T$, the RGN $f_T^{RGN}$ learns to map past principal rays from $f_{T-1}^{RGN}$ and current GT principal rays [S27]. During inference, it randomly selects a number $x \in [0, 1]$ and generates a principal ray using RGN, then uses camera intrinsic $K$ to randomly generate non-principal rays around the principal rays, forming a cone emitted from the camera center [S27]. The network architecture has 3 hidden layers with 16, 64, and 32 hidden units respectively [S27]. Estimated camera poses accumulate errors over tasks, but the generated approximate rays still effectively direct to the scene of interest near past task cameras, so exact reconstruction of past rays is not required [S27]. Unlike methods that reproduce exact past rays, RGN creates approximate rays directed to the scene of interest, allowing errors under a certain level to reduce the learning burden at a small accuracy cost [S27].
 
